mage_only":false}},"duplicate_group":"duplicate-5bca9b4e00754fa5"}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"ibd-f3a8fcdae81984dc-q34","question_number":"34","source":{"course":"Physics","level":"HL","year":2010,"session":"May","paper":1,"paper_label":"1","syllabus":"legacy"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["B.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"For each joule of useful output energy, a gas-powered turbine releases less carbon dioxide than an oil-powered turbine. Explain why this can be an environmental reason for preferring the gas-powered turbine.","assets":[{"path":"practice/parts/ibd-f3a8fcdae81984dc-q34-whole-6813afdefee1cda2.webp","pdf_page":18,"crop_pdf_points_top_left":[35,63.87,525.28,302.18],"dpi":144,"source_sha256":"f2e232011200e1016760238f948c1d3d562294d0585645947a66c10437cd1dd8"}],"answer":{"status":"editorial-solution","text":"Less CO₂ per unit of useful energy means a smaller addition to atmospheric greenhouse gases and a smaller contribution to the enhanced greenhouse effect, assuming the other effects are equal.","pdf_page":2,"source_id":"ibd-0b3e93fe6a3fd1a7","assets":[],"source":{"id":"ibd-0b3e93fe6a3fd1a7","canonical_id":"ibd-0b3e93fe6a3fd1a7","path":"papers/legacy/HL/2010/May/2010-May_Physics-HL_P1_TZ2_markscheme_ibd-0b3e93fe6a3fd1a7.pdf","sha256":"ca90d5d50b9ee2113773d101fdaa8859f9191d191328c2b71a433f4e7ffac8c9","course":"Physics","level":"HL","year":2010,"session":"May","paper":1,"paper_section":"","syllabus":"legacy","option":"","timezone":"TZ2","source_page":"https://ibdocs.re/documents/physics-paper-1-tz2-hl-markscheme-0b3e93fe6a3fd1a7","source_record_ids":["ibd-0b3e93fe6a3fd1a7","fep-33b64d5a643aef5a"],"source_variant":"exam-pack","pages":2,"url":"https://ibdocs.re/p/IB%20PAST%20PAPERS%20-%20YEAR/2010%20Examination%20Session/May%202010%20Examination%20Session/Group%204%20-%20Sciences/Physics_paper_1_TZ2_HL_markscheme.pdf","paper_label":"1","calculator":"unverified","image_only":false}}}],"contexts":[]}],"guidance":{"entries":{"duplicate-31316913e766a637":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"ibd-156cd4cef22f7d6e-q8:a":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"duplicate-814a768cde819a23":{"hint":"Compare the wavelengths of incoming sunlight and radiation emitted by the cooler surface.","notes":["Greenhouse gases absorb and emit selected infrared wavelengths associated with molecular energy changes. Much incoming shortwave sunlight passes through the atmosphere.","After an increase in infrared absorption, the planet warms until outgoing energy again matches absorbed sunlight. Avoid describing the greenhouse effect as reflection of all heat or destruction of energy."]},"duplicate-c41696ff092d4982":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice.","For “show that”, start from the supplied data and display the intermediate steps. Do not 