ource of the released nuclear energy."]},"duplicate-fd6e0c9a76b58ae3":{"hint":"Find the difference between the two relevant energy levels, not the energy of just one level.","notes":["For an atomic transition, photon energy is |E_final − E_initial| = hf = hc/λ. Downward transitions emit; upward transitions require absorption.","Use the positive magnitude of the gap, converting eV to J if using h in SI units. A larger gap corresponds to a shorter wavelength."]},"duplicate-27a3d90b61333688":{"hint":"Check which way surface temperature increases on the horizontal axis before locating the star.","notes":["On a standard H–R diagram luminosity increases upwards and temperature increases to the left. Red giants are luminous but cool; white dwarfs are hot but faint.","Use L = 4πR²σT⁴ to compare radii. A hotter star is not necessarily more luminous, because emitting area also matters."]},"duplicate-ae50fe7451e4501e":{"hint":"Consider both the repulsion between nuclei and the chance of collisions while the fuel is confined.","notes":["Fusion combines light nuclei into more tightly bound products. High temperature helps nuclei approach, high density increases collision rates, and confinement time allows reactions to occur.","Energy release comes from the mass difference or increase in total binding energy. High temperature is a condition for fusion; it is not the source of the released nuclear energy."]},"duplicate-1d882d63aed3d76f":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"duplicate-ce78ab0ac3739484":{"hint":"Compare the wavelengths of incoming sunlight and radiation emitted by the cooler surface.","